IS  - 1532-3064 (Electronic)
IS  - 0954-6111 (Linking)
VI  - 246
DP  - 2025 Sep
TI  - Endobronchial ultrasound-guided transbronchial needle aspiration in real-life 
      settings.
PG  - 108239
LID - S0954-6111(25)00302-6 [pii]
LID - 10.1016/j.rmed.2025.108239 [doi]
AB  - BACKGROUND: Endobronchial ultrasound-transbronchial needle aspiration (EBUS-TBNA) 
      is an important method in staging lung cancer and diagnosing diseases causing 
      mediastinal lymphadenopathy. Most studies confirming the performance of EBUS-TBNA 
      have been prospective studies with strict protocols. This study aimed to evaluate 
      the diagnostic accuracy in real life in a tertiary-level hospital. METHODS: A 
      retrospective evaluation was made of patients who underwent transbronchial needle 
      aspiration (TBNA) with endobronchial ultrasound (EBUS) between January 2019 and 
      December 2023. The results obtained from invasive surgery performed on patients 
      with a negative result were compared with the EBUS-TBNA results. RESULTS: 
      Evaluation was made of 279 patients, comprising 177 males and 102 females with a 
      mean age of 60.11 years (range, 20-87 years). A total of 569 lymph node stations 
      were sampled. The diagnostic sensitivity of the EBUS-TBNA procedure was 
      determined to be 86.4 %, the negative predictive value was 63 %, and the 
      diagnostic success of EBUS-TBNA based on a definitive diagnosis was 89.2 %. 
      CONCLUSION: As the EBUS-TBNA procedure becomes more widespread, there is a need 
      for evaluation in the field other than in specialized centers. The performance of 
      EBUS-TBNA in our center was found to have a base accuracy of 89.2 % for all 
      pathologies. As it is minimally invasive, is reliable, and has high diagnostic 
      rates, it is worth promoting broader applications of EBUS-TBNA in thoracic 
      lesions in non-specialized centers.
