VI  - 251
DP  - 2026 Jan
TI  - Beyond echoes: Interpreting the septated appearance of lymph nodes in sarcoidosis 
      with endobronchial ultrasound.
PG  - 108584
LID - S0954-6111(25)00647-X [pii]
LID - 10.1016/j.rmed.2025.108584 [doi]
AB  - AIM: To differentiate between various diagnoses of TB, sarcoidosis, and 
      malignancy by looking at echoic features of mediastinal lymph nodes during 
      endobronchial ultrasound (EBUS). METHOD: 126 patients underwent EBUS, and 208 
      mediastinal lymph nodes were sampled. EBUS images were reviewed by 3 different 
      individuals blinded to the final diagnosis. Features such as size, shape, 
      margins, echogenicity (heterogeneous or homogeneous), coagulation necrosis sign, 
      and other notable features were observed and compared. RESULT: Out of 208 lymph 
      nodes, 60/208 (28.8 %) nodes had a diagnosis of TB, 62/208 (29.8 %) had 
      sarcoidosis, 30/208 (14.4 %) had malignancy, and 56/208 (26.9 %) were reactive 
      nodes. A distinct septated appearance was noted in 54.9 % (34/62) of lymph nodes 
      in sarcoidosis, while none of the TB lymph nodes had such a feature. 
      Heterogeneous echogenicity was seen in tubercular nodes (46.7 %) compared to 
      sarcoidosis lymph nodes (32.2 %). Coagulation necrosis sign was seen prominently 
      in TB (63.3 %, 38/60) and in only 4 of 62 lymph nodes with sarcoidosis. Granular 
      appearance was noted in 45.2 % (28/62) of sarcoidosis cases, whereas only 10 % 
      (6/60) of TB nodes showed this feature. CONCLUSION: The septated appearance of 
      lymph nodes on EBUS can be helpful to predict sarcoidosis, while heterogeneous 
      echotexture and coagulation necrosis in the lymph nodes on EBUS are suggestive of 
      TB.
