DP  - 2024
TI  - A Novel Procedure for Endobronchial Ultrasound-Guided Transbronchial Mediastinal 
      Cryobiopsy with a Puncture Dilation Catheter.
PG  - 701-706
LID - 10.1159/000540645 [doi]
AB  - INTRODUCTION: Endobronchial ultrasound (EBUS)-guided transbronchial mediastinal 
      cryobiopsy (TBMC) is increasingly used to diagnose mediastinal lymphadenopathy. 
      Various methods have been used to create a tunnel between the airway wall and the 
      lesions for this procedure, such as electrocautery and penetration with the 
      sheath of the needle for EBUS-transbronchial fine needle aspiration. However, 
      those methods are complex. CASE PRESENTATION: We developed a new technique called 
      EBUS-TBMC via a tunnel, and we used it in four cases of mediastinal and/or hilar 
      lymphadenopathy. We used a puncture dilation catheter to create a tunnel between 
      the airway wall and the target lymph node. The cryoprobe was introduced to the 
      target lymph node and cooled with liquid carbon dioxide for 5-9 s. The probe was 
      subsequently pulled out with the samples to complete the EBUS-TBMC via a tunnel. 
      A definite diagnosis was made based on pathological examination of the samples 
      obtained in all four cases. After the procedure, none of the patients experienced 
      moderate to severe bleeding, pneumothorax, pneumomediastinum, or other adverse 
      events. CONCLUSION: EBUS-TBMC via a tunnel is a feasible and convenient procedure 
      for the performance of TBMC. Further studies are required to evaluate the safety 
      and efficacy of EBUS-TBMC via a tunnel.
