TI  - Molecular Analysis of EBUS-TBNA Samples for Nodal Staging in Non-Small Cell Lung 
      Cancer.
LID - 10.3390/cancers18111797 [doi]
LID - 1797
AB  - Since the discovery of EGFR mutations, treatment strategies have undergone major 
      advances over the past two decades. Accordingly, molecular testing for oncogenic 
      driver mutations is recommended at the time of diagnosis of advanced NSCLC. In 
      addition, molecular analysis of lymph node samples has emerged as a promising 
      tool for improving the accuracy of nodal staging in NSCLC. This approach may 
      enhance the precision of staging in early-stage disease planned for curative 
      surgical resection, thereby optimizing adjuvant treatment decisions and 
      supporting complete resection at the time of surgery. In this review, we examine 
      the importance and current status of molecular testing for oncogenic driver 
      mutations in samples obtained via endobronchial ultrasound-guided transbronchial 
      needle aspiration (EBUS-TBNA) or EBUS-guided cryobiopsy, and its role in 
      improving the accuracy of nodal staging in NSCLC.
