TI  - The Role of Endobronchial Ultrasound in Early-Stage Non-Small Cell Lung Cancer.
PG  - 122
AB  - Aim The aim of this study is to assess the impact of EBUS on the concordance of 
      clinical and pathological NSCLC staging in our center. Methods Data was collected 
      retrospectively from the hospital database regarding patients who underwent 
      surgical resection for early stage NSCLC between 2012 and 2017. Results A total 
      of 251 patients were included. The mean age was 67 (±9), 55% (n=137) were male 
      and 83% (n=209) were current/former smokers. In group A (n=154, 61%) clinical 
      nodal stage (cN) was established from a combination of CT, PET CT and 
      mediastinoscopy. Group B underwent additional EBUS (n=97, 39%). cN and 
      pathological nodal staging (pN) were concordant in 78% (n=120) in group A versus 
      62% (n=60) in group B (p=0.009). Conclusion This study demonstrated higher rates 
      of nodal discordance in patients who underwent EBUS which contrasts existing data 
      that demonstrates improved concordance with EBUS. We describe these findings and 
      potential explanations further in this study.
