DP  - 2022 Oct 20
TI  - Prediction of malignant lymph nodes in NSCLC by machine-learning classifiers 
      using EBUS-TBNA and PET/CT.
PG  - 17511
LID - 10.1038/s41598-022-21637-y [doi]
LID - 17511
AB  - Accurate determination of lymph-node (LN) metastases is a prerequisite for high 
      precision radiotherapy. The primary aim is to characterise the performance of 
      PET/CT-based machine-learning classifiers to predict LN-involvement by 
      endobronchial ultrasound-guided transbronchial needle aspiration (EBUS-TBNA) in 
      stage-III NSCLC. Prediction models for LN-positivity based on [(18)F]FDG-PET/CT 
      features were built using logistic regression and machine-learning models random 
      forest (RF) and multilayer perceptron neural network (MLP) for stage-III NSCLC 
      before radiochemotherapy. A total of 675 LN-stations were sampled in 180 
      patients. The logistic and RF models identified SUV(max), the short-axis 
      LN-diameter and the echelon of the considered LN among the most important 
      parameters for EBUS-positivity. Adjusting the sensitivity of machine-learning 
      classifiers to that of the expert-rater of 94.5%, MLP (P = 0.0061) and RF models 
      (P = 0.038) showed lower misclassification rates (MCR) than the standard-report, 
      weighting false positives and false negatives equally. Increasing the sensitivity 
      of classifiers from 94.5 to 99.3% resulted in increase of MCR from 13.3/14.5 to 
      29.8/34.2% for MLP/RF, respectively. PET/CT-based machine-learning classifiers 
      can achieve a high sensitivity (94.5%) to detect EBUS-positive LNs at a low 
      misclassification rate. As the specificity decreases rapidly above that level, a 
      combined test of a PET/CT-based MLP/RF classifier and EBUS-TBNA is recommended 
      for radiation target volume definition.
