DP  - 2020 Jul
TI  - A rare disease mimicking lung cancer.
PG  - 430-432
LID - S0019-5707(20)30045-7 [pii]
LID - 10.1016/j.ijtb.2020.05.004 [doi]
AB  - We present an interesting case of a 34 year old gentleman with bilateral FDG avid 
      lesions in the apex of the lung. He presented with a two month history of cough, 
      fever and hemoptysis. A contrast enhanced CT scan showed a spiculated lesion in 
      the right upper lobe. A CT guided biopsy was done which was suggestive of an 
      inflammatory lesion. He was started empirically on anti tubercular therapy, but 
      his symptoms did not subside. A PET CT scan was done, which showed the lesion to 
      be FDG avid. A repeat biopsy was done and again it was reported as an 
      inflammatory infiltrate. He was then referred to our center for a surgical 
      biopsy. We repeated a PET scan and were surprised to find another lesion in the 
      apex of the opposite lung. The case was discussed with our interventional 
      pulmonogy team and an EBUS guided biopsy of the lesion was done. On 
      microbiological analysis the specimen was found to be positive for Nocardia.
