TI  - Histoplasma capsulatum Infection With Both Granulomatous Features and Fibrosing 
      Mediastinitis Presenting as Shortness of Breath: A Case Report.
PG  - e70398
LID - 10.7759/cureus.70398 [doi]
LID - e70398
AB  - It is known that Histoplasma capsulatum can cause chronic granulomatous disease 
      or fibrosing mediastinitis, but both presentations occurring in the same patient 
      is exceedingly rare and difficult to diagnose. The patient is a 24-year-old 
      female with a past medical history of asthma, who presented for worsening 
      shortness of breath. Thoracic imaging revealed a large paratracheal mass with a 
      significant mass effect. Endobronchial ultrasound (EBUS) was attempted 
      unsuccessfully, and the patient underwent video-assisted thoracoscopic surgery, 
      which established the diagnosis of fibrosing mediastinitis. Extensive infectious 
      disease workup confirmed Histoplasma infection. She was treated with a course of 
      Itraconazole, pulmonary artery stenting, and rituximab.
