TI  - PD-L1(+) lung cancer stem cells modify the metastatic lymph-node 
      immunomicroenvironment in nsclc patients.
PG  - 453-461
LID - 10.1007/s00262-020-02648-y [doi]
AB  - INTRODUCTION: Cancer stem cells (CSCs) are implicated in tumor initiation and 
      development of metastasis. However, whether CSCs also affect the immune system is 
      not fully understood. We investigated correlations between the PD-L1(+) CSCs, 
      changes in T-cell phenotype in metastatic and non-metastatic lymph nodes (LNs) 
      and response to treatment. METHODS: LNs' aspirates were obtained during the 
      EBUS/TBNA procedure of 20 NSCLC patients at different stages of the disease. CSCs 
      and T-cell characteristics were determined by flow cytometry. RESULTS: PD-L1(+) 
      CSCs positively correlated with the percentage of Tregs, PD-1(+) CD4 T cells and 
      Tim3(+) CD4(+) T cells, whereas PD-L1(+) CSCs were negatively correlated with 
      CD4(+) T cells and CD28(+) CD4(+) T cells. The percentage of PD-L1(+) CSCs was 
      higher in patients with progressive disease (PD) as compared to patients with 
      stable disease (SD) or partial response (PR). Among T cells, only PD-1(+) CD4(+) 
      T cells and Tim3(+) CD4(+) T-cell frequencies were higher in patients with PD as 
      compared to patients with SD or PR. CONCLUSION: The frequency of PD-L1(+) CSCs 
      associates with an altered T-cell frequency and phenotype indicating that CSCs 
      can affect the immune system. The higher percentage of PD-L1(+) CSCs in patients 
      with PD may confirm their resistance to conventional therapy, suggesting that 
      CSCs may be an interesting target for immunotherapy.
