TI  - The Detection of Lung Cancer Cell Profiles in Mediastinal Lymph Nodes Using a 
      Hematological Analyzer and Flow Cytometry Method.
LID - 10.3390/cancers17030431 [doi]
LID - 431
AB  - The presence of metastases in mediastinal lymph nodes (LNs) is essential for 
      planning lung cancer treatment and assessing anticancer immune responses. The aim 
      of the study was to assess LNs for the presence of neoplastic cells and evaluate 
      lung cancer-selected antigen expression. LN aspirates were obtained during an 
      EBUS/TBNA procedure. The cells were analyzed using a hematological analyzer and 
      flow cytometry. It was possible to indicate the presence of cells characterized 
      by high fluorescence connected with high metabolic activity using a hematological 
      analyzer and to determine their non-hematopoietic origin using flow cytometry. 
      Using these methods together, we detected very quickly a high proportion of 
      cancer cells in LNs. We noticed that it was possible to determine a high 
      expression of EpCAM, TTF-1, Ki67, cytokeratin, HER, and differences between 
      non-small-cell (NSCLC) and small-cell lung cancer (SCLC) for the antigens MUC-1, 
      CD56, HLA-DR, CD39, CD184, PD-L1, PD-L2 and CTLA-4 on tumor cells. We report, for 
      the first time, that the detection of tumor cells in LNs with the expression of 
      specific antigens is easy to evaluate using a hematological analyzer and flow 
      cytometry in EBUS/TBNA samples. Such precise characteristics of non-hematopoietic 
      cells in LNs may be of great diagnostic importance in the detection of 
      micrometastases.
