DP  - 2009 Dec
TI  - Mediastinal staging in lung cancer: a rational approach.
PG  - 170-5
AB  - Mediastinal staging is generally performed by the use of imaging techniques such 
      as CT (Computed Tomography) and PET (Positron Emission Tomography), mini-invasive 
      techniques, as TBNA (Transbronchial Needle Aspiration), EBUS-TBNA 
      (Ultrasound-Guided Transbronchial Needle Aspiration), EUS-FNA (Endoscopic Ultra 
      Sound Fine-Needle Aspiration), and/or surgical techniques as mediastinoscopy, 
      thoracoscopy, mediastinothomy. Each of these techniques provides different 
      sensitivity, specificity and predictive value: all these characteristics need to 
      be well considered and adequately used to achieve the best possible outcome, best 
      exploitation of available resources and least discomfort for the patient. 
      Particularly, indicators which may suggest the need for further examination of 
      mediastinum, following a negative CT and PET, will be discussed in this review; 
      need for surgical confirmation after negative TBNA will be considered, also.
