TI  - Highly Sensitive Detection of miR-200c in Metastatic Lymph Nodes Using Scanning 
      Single-Molecule Counting.
LID - 10.3390/cancers17193133 [doi]
LID - 3133
AB  - BACKGROUND/OBJECTIVES: Endobronchial ultrasound-guided transbronchial needle 
      aspiration (EBUS-TBNA) is a well-established, minimally invasive method for 
      sampling mediastinal and hilar lymph nodes. It has high sensitivity and 
      specificity for diagnosing lymph node involvement. However, achieving a 
      definitive cytopathological diagnosis can be challenging owing to the limited 
      presence of atypical tumor cells. The scanning single-molecule counting (SSMC) 
      method enables rapid measurement of target molecule expression. This study 
      assessed the correlation between miR-200c expression measured by SSMC and lymph 
      node metastasis confirmed by cytopathology. METHODS: Following EBUS-TBNA in 
      patients with lung cancer or suspected lung cancer, we flushed 22-gauge biopsy 
      needles with 1 mL saline and extracted microRNA (miRNA) from the lavage fluid. 
      The quality of SSMC results for miR-200c was evaluated and compared with that of 
      quantitative real-time polymerase chain reaction (RT-qPCR). RESULTS: Linear 
      regression analysis of miR-200c between SSMC and RT-qPCR showed a statistically 
      significant positive correlation (R(2) = 0.81, p < 0.0001), demonstrating the 
      feasibility of SSMC for evaluating miRNA in lymph nodes. Based on these findings, 
      we enrolled and analyzed 100 lymph nodes from 86 patients. High miR-200c 
      expression detected lymph node metastasis with high sensitivity (85.7%) and 
      specificity (83.3%) and an AUC of 0.88. No discordance was observed between the 
      standard SSMC and rapid methods. CONCLUSIONS: The expression of miR-200c, as 
      measured by SSMC, may serve as a biomarker for molecular nodal staging. The rapid 
      SSMC method provides results within 30 min, significantly faster than 
      conventional RT-qPCR, and may complement rapid on-site cytological evaluation.
