TI  - Peripheral pulmonary carcinoid tumor diagnosed by endobronchial-ultrasound-guided 
      bronchoscopy.
PG  - 10-2
LID - 10.1002/rcr2.139 [doi]
AB  - A 45-year-old Japanese woman complained of uncontrolled hypertension and face 
      swelling. She was diagnosed with Cushing's syndrome with secretion of 
      adrenocorticotropic hormone. Fluorodeoxyglucose positron emission 
      tomography-computed tomography revealed a 2 × 2 cm mass in her left lung, with 
      high standardized maximum uptake value. She underwent bronchoscopy with 
      endobronchial ultrasound via a guide-sheath. Surgical resection of her left upper 
      lung was performed, and pathological examination showed a typical carcinoid 
      tumor. After lung resection, she recovered from her subjective symptoms. 
      Diagnosis of peripheral carcinoid tumor of the lung is generally difficult. Here, 
      we introduce a case of peripheral pulmonary carcinoid tumor diagnosed by 
      endobronchial-ultrasound-guided bronchoscopy.
