DP  - 2021 Aug
TI  - Mediastinal lymph node metastasis as a single expression of disease relapse in 
      Ewing's sarcoma: multidisciplinary approach of two consecutive cases.
PG  - 214-219
LID - 10.5582/irdr.2021.01063 [doi]
AB  - Ewing's sarcoma of the bone is a rare, highly aggressive tumor that typically 
      affects children and young adults. Progress in the treatment of Ewing's sarcoma 
      has improved survival from about 10%, before the introduction of chemotherapy, to 
      about 75% today for patients with localized tumors. On the contrary, metastatic 
      disease still has a poor prognosis, and a multidisciplinary approach is essential 
      to improve the outcome. Molecular techniques and new imaging modalities are 
      affecting the diagnosis and classification of patients with Ewing's sarcoma. The 
      most frequent sites of metastases in Ewing's sarcoma include lungs, bones and 
      bone marrow. Lymph nodes are a rare site of metastatic spread, particularly in 
      the mediastinum. In this report, we present two consecutive cases of patients 
      with Ewing's Sarcoma, diagnosed, and treated at our institute. We focused 
      particularly on the rarity of the atypical presentation of the disease and on the 
      synergistic strategy to adopt as a model of networking in treating patients with 
      rare diseases.
