TI  - The "Galaxy Sign" in Lung Cancer Screening: A Benign Mimic of Malignancy.
PG  - e109164
LID - 10.7759/cureus.109164 [doi]
LID - e109164
AB  - The "galaxy sign" is a distinctive radiologic pattern characterized by a central 
      confluent pulmonary opacity surrounded by smaller satellite nodules, most 
      commonly associated with sarcoidosis, among other granulomatous and inflammatory 
      conditions. Recognition of this finding is important in lung cancer screening 
      populations, where indeterminate nodules may prompt concern for malignancy. A 
      69-year-old ex-smoker underwent low-dose CT screening, which demonstrated a 60 mm 
      cluster of dense nodules with a central 20 mm focus in the left upper lobe. 
      Fluorodeoxyglucose (FDG) PET showed mild uptake (SUVmax 3.1) within the lesion 
      and symmetrical, FDG-avid mediastinal and hilar lymphadenopathy (SUVmax 12.5). 
      Endobronchial ultrasound-guided biopsy of a mediastinal lymph node revealed 
      fibrosis and burnt-out granulomatous inflammation without malignancy or 
      infection. Correlation of imaging and histology supported pulmonary sarcoidosis. 
      Awareness of the galaxy sign may help distinguish benign granulomatous disease 
      from malignancy in screening cohorts and guide appropriate multidisciplinary 
      management.
