TI  - Diagnostic accuracy and safety of 1.1-mm cryoprobe biopsy for peripheral 
      pulmonary lesions with or without oversheath.
PG  - 138
LID - 10.21037/tlcr-2026-1-0152 [doi]
LID - 138
AB  - BACKGROUND: Cryobiopsy using a 1.1-mm probe enables the acquisition of larger and 
      better-preserved tissue specimens for peripheral pulmonary lesions (PPLs). 
      However, the effects of oversheathed cryobiopsy on diagnostic performance and 
      procedural safety remain uncertain. This study aimed to evaluate whether the use 
      of an oversheath influences diagnostic performance and procedural safety during 
      bronchoscopic biopsy of PPLs using a 1.1-mm cryoprobe. METHODS: This 
      single-center retrospective study included 85 patients who underwent 
      bronchoscopic biopsy for PPLs ≤30 mm in diameter using a 1.1-mm cryoprobe between 
      March 2022 and August 2023. Patients were categorized into two groups according 
      to oversheath use: procedures performed with an oversheath (n=39) and those 
      performed without an oversheath (n=46). Diagnostic success rates, and 
      procedure-related complications were evaluated, with particular focus on bleeding 
      events. RESULTS: Cryobiopsy using a 1.1-mm probe achieved a significantly higher 
      diagnostic yield compared with that via forceps biopsy (70.6% vs. 39.2%), 
      resulting in an overall diagnostic success rate of 76.5%. Diagnostic yield did 
      not differ significantly between procedures performed with and without an 
      oversheath. However, the incidence of moderate-to-severe bleeding was 
      significantly lower in procedures performed with an oversheath (25.6% vs. 50.0%). 
      Subgroup analysis demonstrated a trend toward a higher diagnostic yield for 
      cryobiopsy with an oversheath in smaller lesions (≤20 mm) compared with that via 
      procedures without an oversheath (80.0% vs. 54.2%). Multivariable analysis 
      identified a 'within' radial endobronchial ultrasound position as an independent 
      predictor of diagnostic success. CONCLUSIONS: Cryobiopsy using a 1.1-mm probe 
      provides a superior diagnostic yield compared with that via forceps biopsy for 
      PPLs. The use of an oversheath during cryobiopsy reduces bleeding complications 
      and may further improve diagnostic performance, particularly in smaller lesions.
